<h1>Curation Reports</h1>

<p>Every report the build produces for a human to judge, in one place. They are all
<strong>advisory</strong>: each one states a disagreement or a gap and stops there. Nothing in
the build reads them back, none of them can fail a build, and none of them causes anything to be
changed or deleted. Acting on a report is always somebody's decision.</p>

<p>This page is not linked from anywhere on the site and is not indexed by search — it is a
working page for the people maintaining the catalogue.</p>

<p>Rulings made in response to these reports belong in the
<a href="{{ repoUrl }}/blob/main/docs/curation-log.md">curation log</a>. Questions still waiting on
the curator are labelled <code>curation</code>
<a href="{{ repoUrl }}/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3Acuration">in the issue tracker</a>.</p>

<h2 id="for-the-curator">For the curator</h2>

<p>These ask questions only someone who knows the moths and the collection can answer — which
species a photograph belongs to, whether two names are the same animal, whether a gap is real.</p>

{% for report in curationReports.curation %}{% include "curation-report.njk" %}{% endfor %}

<h2 id="data-quality">Data quality</h2>

<p>These are for whoever is maintaining the data. They compare two fallible sources against each
other — a typed county against a coordinate, a record against a schema — and report where the two
disagree, without preferring either.</p>

{% for report in curationReports.engineering %}{% include "curation-report.njk" %}{% endfor %}

<h2 id="reading-these-files">Reading these files</h2>

<p>Almost every report is a plain CSV or JSON file. CSVs open directly in Excel, Numbers or Google
Sheets; a few are large enough that a spreadsheet is the only comfortable way to read them. The
files are regenerated as described above and replaced wholesale each time, so a copy you have
downloaded is a snapshot, not a live view.</p>

<p>One report — link rot — has no file. Its findings go to a GitHub issue that closes itself when
the links recover, because a link that fails once from a build server has usually not failed at
all, and a file full of those gets read once and never again. What is listed there has failed
twice, a week apart.</p>

<p>The columns of each underlying data file are documented in
<a href="{{ repoUrl }}/blob/main/data/README.md">data/README.md</a>.</p>

